[49m[38;5;10m+      1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # Differential verification of the C → Rust translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reference: `c_src/src/main.c` + `c_src/src/lib.c`, built with CMake to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c_src/build/driver`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Translation: `translation/src/main.rs` + `translation/src/lib.rs`, built with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cargo build --release` to `translation/target/release/driver`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both programs are driven **as executables**: stdin is piped in, and stdout,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr and the exit status are compared byte for byte. The Rust code is never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m loaded as a library. The harness lives in `tests/differential.rs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **No behavioural mismatch was found.** Every input class enumerated below
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m produces identical stdout, identical stderr and an identical exit status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This document records what was checked rather than a list of repairs, because
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m no repair to `translation/` was required. The sections below name the places
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m where a mismatch was plausible, what the C actually does there, and how it was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m confirmed — so the next reader can re-check the same ground instead of taking
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "it passed" on trust.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## How much was compared
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Sweep | Cases | Mismatches |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `cargo test` suite (24 tests, `tests/differential.rs`) | ~34,000 stdin streams | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Exhaustive y/n strings, length 0–13, operations 2 and 3 | 32,766 | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Random y/n strings, length 14–80, operations 0–3 | 6,000 | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `atoi`/`strtol` boundary matrix on both numeric lines | 16,625 | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Random raw byte streams (whole stdin is garbage) | 2,700 | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Structured + boundary-length sweeps around the 1024-byte buffer | ~55,000 | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No test is `#[ignore]`d, disabled or skipped. Nothing in `c_src/` was modified;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the harness builds the C program out-of-tree into `translation/target/c_build`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m when `c_src/build/driver` is absent, so a test run never writes into `c_src/`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours that had to match exactly, and were verified
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `fgets`, not `scanf` — line-oriented reads with a hard 1024-byte cap
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` calls `fgets(input_buffer, 1024, stdin)` three times. `fgets` stops
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m after at most 1023 bytes **and leaves the remainder in the stream**. So a first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m line of 1030 `0` characters is consumed as the *operation* (first 1023 bytes)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and its tail becomes the *parameter* line. A naive `read_line` translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m would consume the whole line and shift every subsequent read.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main.rs` reimplements `fgets` over a `BufRead` with `fill_buf`/`consume`, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unread bytes stay queued. Confirmed by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `lines_longer_than_the_1024_byte_buffer`, which walks lengths 1020–1026 and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2048 for the decision line and 1030 for the operation and parameter lines.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. Three distinct EOF error paths, each exiting 1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each `fgets` returning `NULL` prints a *different* message to **stderr** and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m returns 1:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - no operation line → `Error reading operation\n`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - no parameter line → `Error reading parameter\n`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - no decision line → `Error reading decision string\n`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A stdout-only comparison would pass while the exit status diverged, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `assert_same` asserts all three channels. Confirmed by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stdin_truncated_at_every_fgets` for stdin of `""`, `"0\n"`, `"0"`, `"0\n0\n"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `"0\n0"`. Note `"0"` (no newline) is a *successful* `fgets`, so it fails at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the second read, not the first.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. `atoi` is `(int) strtol(..., 10)` — it saturates, then truncates
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This was the highest-risk spot. glibc's `atoi` skips leading whitespace,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m accepts one sign, stops at the first non-digit, **saturates at `long` bounds**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (64-bit here) and then **truncates to `int`**. Consequences that a plain
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `str::parse::<i32>()` would get wrong:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `"4294967296"` → `0` (truncation), not a parse error
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `"9223372036854775808"` → saturates to `LONG_MAX`, truncates to `-1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `"-9223372036854775809"` → saturates to `LONG_MIN`, truncates to `0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `"12abc"` → `12`; `"abc"` → `0`; `"0x1f"` → `0`; `"3.99"` → `3`; `"007"` → `7`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `""` and `" "` → `0`, which selects operation 0, not an error
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `atoi` in `main.rs` accumulates in `i64` with `checked_mul`/`checked_add`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m latches an overflow flag, saturates to `i64::MIN`/`MAX`, then casts with `as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m i32`. Confirmed by `atoi_saturates_like_strtol_then_truncates_to_int` (a full
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 23×23 matrix of boundary values across both numeric lines) and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `atoi_parsing_of_operation_and_parameter`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. Only a *trailing* newline is stripped, and `strlen` defines the length
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` computes `len = strlen(input_buffer)` and strips one trailing `'\n'`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two consequences:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - An embedded NUL byte truncates the decision string. `"y\0nn"` has `len == 1`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so operation 2 sees a single decision and returns `1001`, not `1002`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - A `\r\n` line ending leaves the `\r` in place as a decision character, where
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `parse_bool` maps it to `false`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Confirmed by `embedded_nul_bytes_truncate_the_decision_string` and the CRLF
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cases in `blank_lines_and_missing_newlines`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. `apply_permissions` has a fall-through that looks like a bug
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m } else if (read && write) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     if (permission_value == 6) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m         return 50 + permission_value;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }                      /* no else -- falls through to `return 0` */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `lib.rs` reproduces this: the `read && write` arm returns `56` only when
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `permission_value == 6` and otherwise falls out of the chain to the final
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `return 0`. (The condition is in fact always true on that arm, since
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `read && write && !execute` forces `permission_value == 6`, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fall-through is dead — but the structure is preserved rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "simplified".) The observed output set for operation 0 is exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `{-20, -10, -2, -1, 0, 14, 23, 35, 56, 107}` for both programs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. `validate_sequence` aliases the caller's `char *` as `bool *`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bool *bools = (bool*)sequence;   /* Reuse buffer */
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m for (size_t i = 0; i < len; i++) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     bool val = parse_bool(sequence[i]);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     bools[i] = val;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The input buffer is overwritten in place with `0`/`1` bytes. This is safe to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m model with a separate `Vec<bool>` only because index `i` is **read before it is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m written** and indices ascend, so no later read ever sees a rewritten byte — and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m because `main` does not touch the buffer again after the call. Had the loop
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m descended, or had it read `sequence[i+1]`, a separate vector would have
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m diverged. `lib.rs` documents this at the function and uses `vec![false; len]`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 7. Signed/unsigned and width details
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `count`, `len` are `size_t`; `special_count`, `transitions` are `int`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Comparisons like `special_count == count` and `transitions == len - 1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   promote the `int` to `size_t`. Both operands are non-negative wherever these
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   run, so the promotion is benign; `lib.rs` casts explicitly and uses
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `count.wrapping_sub(1)` for `count - 1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `configure_flags` caps at 32 decisions (`count = min(length, 32)`), so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   40-character all-`y` string returns `1032`, not `1040`. Covered by the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   32-element cap cases.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `1u << i` is only evaluated for `i < 32`, so no undefined shift occurs. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   resulting `flags` bitmask is computed and **never read** in the C; `lib.rs`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   keeps the computation and discards it with `let _ = flags;`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - No arithmetic in the translation can overflow an `i32`, so the release build
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (wrapping) and the debug build (overflow panics) behave identically. This
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   matters because `cargo test` builds the dev profile.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 8. Write-failure behaviour
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C's `printf`/`fprintf` ignore write errors, whereas Rust's `print!`/`eprint!`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m panic on them. Checked with stdout closed (`1>&-`) and stderr closed (`2>&-`):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m both programs still exited 0 and 1 respectively, so no divergence is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m observable. `main.rs` also ignores the result of its explicit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stdout().flush()`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Branches in the C that no input can reach
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Enumerated while looking for untested paths. These are dead in the C, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m absence of a test for them is a property of the source, not a gap in the suite.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `lib.rs` still mirrors each one structurally.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
