[0m[0m
The places where a mismatch was most plausible, all confirmed already handled correctly in the existing Rust:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mfgets[0m, not [38;5;10mread_line[0m** — the 1023-byte cap means a long first line is split across the operation [3mand[23m parameter reads, with the tail left queued in the stream. [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m reimplements this over [38;5;10mfill_buf[0m/[38;5;10mconsume[0m.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10matoi[0m is [38;5;10m(int) strtol(...)[0m** — saturates at 64-bit [38;5;10mlong[0m bounds then truncates, so [38;5;10m"4294967296"[0m → [38;5;10m0[0m, [38;5;10m"9223372036854775808"[0m → [38;5;10m-1[0m, [38;5;10m"abc"[0m → [38;5;10m0[0m, [38;5;10m""[0m → [38;5;10m0[0m (which selects operation 0, not an error). A [38;5;10mparse::<i32>()[0m translation would have diverged on all of these.[0m[0m
- **Three distinct stderr messages, each exiting 1** — a stdout-only comparison would have passed while the exit status differed.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mstrlen[0m defines the length**, so an embedded NUL truncates the decision string ([38;5;10m"y\0nn"[0m → [38;5;10m1001[0m, not [38;5;10m1002[0m).[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mapply_permissions[0m falls through to [38;5;10mreturn 0[0m** with no [38;5;10melse[0m after the [38;5;10mread && write[0m arm; preserved rather than simplified.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mvalidate_sequence[0m aliases the caller's [38;5;10mchar*[0m as [38;5;10mbool*[0m** and overwrites it in place. A separate [38;5;10mVec<bool>[0m is only equivalent because each index is read before it is written and indices ascend — noted in [38;5;10mERRORS.md[0m as the reason it is safe here.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I also confirmed the suite can actually fail: three mutated Rust binaries (wrong return value, wrong exit code, reworded stderr) were each caught, on the channel they corrupted. Without that check, 24 green tests would have been unfalsified.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
